Here's the approach: use ASP.NET Core's built-in `IExceptionHandler` (available in .NET 8+) combined with `AddProblemDetails()`. This intercepts all unhandled exceptions globally.

**1. Create the exception handler:**

```csharp
// GlobalExceptionHandler.cs
using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Diagnostics;
using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Mvc;

public class GlobalExceptionHandler : IExceptionHandler
{
    private readonly ILogger<GlobalExceptionHandler> _logger;

    public GlobalExceptionHandler(ILogger<GlobalExceptionHandler> logger)
    {
        _logger = logger;
    }

    public async ValueTask<bool> TryHandleAsync(
        HttpContext httpContext,
        Exception exception,
        CancellationToken cancellationToken)
    {
        var (statusCode, title) = exception switch
        {
            KeyNotFoundException => (StatusCodes.Status404NotFound, "Not Found"),
            ArgumentException => (StatusCodes.Status400BadRequest, "Bad Request"),
            InvalidOperationException => (StatusCodes.Status409Conflict, "Conflict"),
            _ => (StatusCodes.Status500InternalServerError, "Internal Server Error")
        };

        _logger.LogError(exception, "Unhandled exception: {Message}", exception.Message);

        var problemDetails = new ProblemDetails
        {
            Status = statusCode,
            Title = title,
            Detail = statusCode == StatusCodes.Status500InternalServerError
                ? "An unexpected error occurred."
                : exception.Message,
            Instance = httpContext.Request.Path
        };

        httpContext.Response.StatusCode = statusCode;
        await httpContext.Response.WriteAsJsonAsync(problemDetails, cancellationToken);
        return true;
    }
}
```

**2. Register in `Program.cs`:**

```csharp
var builder = WebApplication.CreateBuilder(args);

// Adds ProblemDetails support for non-exception error responses too
builder.Services.AddProblemDetails();

// Register the global handler
builder.Services.AddExceptionHandler<GlobalExceptionHandler>();

var app = builder.Build();

// Must come before endpoint mapping
app.UseExceptionHandler();

// Your endpoints...
app.MapGet("/items/{id}", (int id) =>
{
    throw new KeyNotFoundException($"Item {id} not found");
});

app.Run();
```

**How it works:**
- `UseExceptionHandler()` catches any unhandled exception from the pipeline
- `IExceptionHandler.TryHandleAsync` maps it to the correct status code and writes a Problem Details JSON response
- For 500s, the actual exception message is suppressed—only a generic message is returned
- No try-catch needed in individual endpoints

**Example 404 response:**
```json
{
  "status": 404,
  "title": "Not Found",
  "detail": "Item 42 not found",
  "instance": "/items/42"
}
```

If you're on .NET 7 (no `IExceptionHandler`), use middleware instead:

```csharp
app.UseExceptionHandler(app => app.Run(async context =>
{
    var exception = context.Features.Get<IExceptionHandlerFeature>()?.Error;
    // same mapping logic here
}));
```
